WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.730
Think about a set of instructions
that made a new task easy.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:08.550
Most likely they told you the next
step and left nothing to guess.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:14.230
Today you will make a small resource
pack for a library computer-help session.

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:16.900
You will write a handout in Word,

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:21.430
total a supply list in Excel,
and build three slides in PowerPoint.

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:27.330
Then you will save the handout as a PDF
and check it the way your reader will.

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:30.270
Every choice starts with the reader.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:35.201
Who will pick up this handout,
and what should they do after reading it?

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:39.630
Write one sentence that names
your reader and their next step.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:41.810
Pause the video now.

00:00:55.917 --> 00:00:59.751 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Open a blank Word document and type
the handout lines from your worksheet.

00:00:59.957 --> 00:01:03.017 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Select the first line,
Computer help at the library.

00:01:04.037 --> 00:01:06.197 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
On the Home tab, choose Heading 1.

00:01:06.837 --> 00:01:10.537 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Then make Who it helps
and Steps into Heading 2.

00:01:10.537 --> 00:01:13.757 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Bold text only changes how a line looks.

00:01:14.437 --> 00:01:18.957 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
A heading style tells Word, and a screen
reader, that a new section starts here.

00:01:19.717 --> 00:01:23.377 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
To check, open the View tab and
turn on the Navigation Pane.

00:01:23.377 --> 00:01:25.957 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Your headings appear
in a list on the left.

00:01:27.017 --> 00:01:29.997 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Next, select the three
steps and choose Numbering.

00:01:30.797 --> 00:01:31.547 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
For the link,

00:01:31.617 --> 00:01:36.377 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
select words that name where it goes,
such as Read the computer-help guide.

00:01:37.497 --> 00:01:39.837 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Press Control and K, and type the address.

00:01:41.772 --> 00:01:44.052
A few keys save a lot of mouse work.

00:01:44.832 --> 00:01:47.192
Control and C copies what you selected.

00:01:47.952 --> 00:01:49.972
Control and V pastes it.

00:01:50.532 --> 00:01:55.772
Control and Z undoes your last change,
so a mistake is easy to take back.

00:01:56.972 --> 00:01:58.427
Now trade seats with a partner.

00:01:59.272 --> 00:02:01.241
In their handout, open the Review tab,

00:02:01.972 --> 00:02:06.612
turn on Track Changes, and change
one word to make a step clearer.

00:02:07.712 --> 00:02:11.752
Word shows your edit in color instead
of quietly replacing the text.

00:02:13.232 --> 00:02:14.332
A comment is different.

00:02:15.012 --> 00:02:17.572
It explains why, and it changes nothing.

00:02:18.532 --> 00:02:22.452
Back at your own seat,
choose Accept or Reject for each change.

00:02:23.392 --> 00:02:25.432
The owner always makes the final call.

00:02:26.792 --> 00:02:30.072
Build your handout,
then trade seats and track one edit.

00:02:31.032 --> 00:02:32.012
Pause the video now.

00:02:46.109 --> 00:02:50.489 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Download supplies.csv from your
worksheet and open it in Excel.

00:02:51.609 --> 00:02:57.169 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Columns have letters and rows have
numbers, so B2 means column B, row 2.

00:02:58.229 --> 00:03:03.609 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
The list has paper at 12 dollars,
folders at 8, and pens at 5.

00:03:04.649 --> 00:03:07.549 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Change the heading in
B1 to Cost in dollars,

00:03:08.129 --> 00:03:10.202 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
so a reader knows what the numbers mean.

00:03:10.869 --> 00:03:13.729 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Click cell B5, just below the costs.

00:03:14.609 --> 00:03:21.609 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Type an equals sign, then SUM, then B2
colon B4 in parentheses, and press Enter.

00:03:23.489 --> 00:03:29.009 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
The equals sign starts a formula, and the
colon means every cell from B2 through B4.

00:03:30.129 --> 00:03:31.409 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
B5 shows 25.

00:03:32.989 --> 00:03:34.854 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Before you change anything, predict.

00:03:35.829 --> 00:03:39.189 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
If paper costs 15 dollars,
what will the total be?

00:03:40.289 --> 00:03:43.501 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Enter the formula in your own
sheet and write your prediction.

00:03:44.529 --> 00:03:45.468 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Pause the video now.

00:03:56.572 --> 00:03:57.746 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Now test your prediction.

00:03:58.252 --> 00:04:01.592 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Click B2, type 15, and press Enter.

00:04:02.692 --> 00:04:04.612 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
The total changes to 28 by itself,

00:04:04.612 --> 00:04:09.272 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
because the formula adds the cells,
not a number someone typed.

00:04:10.612 --> 00:04:15.592 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
If your total stayed at 25,
click B5 and read the formula bar.

00:04:16.492 --> 00:04:20.432 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
A typed number, or a range that
skips a cell, will not update.

00:04:21.212 --> 00:04:23.432 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
When it works, save it the right way.

00:04:24.092 --> 00:04:28.512 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Choose File, then Save As,
and pick Excel Workbook as the type.

00:04:29.512 --> 00:04:32.472 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
A CSV file keeps the numbers
but drops the formula.

00:04:33.812 --> 00:04:34.992 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Change paper to 15,

00:04:35.972 --> 00:04:39.806 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
check the total against your prediction,
and save it as an Excel workbook.

00:04:40.832 --> 00:04:41.952 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Pause the video now.

00:04:56.103 --> 00:04:58.863
Slides work best when
each one does one job.

00:04:59.883 --> 00:05:02.663
The first slide names the need:
Need computer help?

00:05:03.643 --> 00:05:04.911
The second shows the steps.

00:05:05.903 --> 00:05:07.223
The third says where to go.

00:05:08.283 --> 00:05:10.663
Use the same three steps
as your handout, so

00:05:10.663 --> 00:05:16.043
your reader gets one message, not two.
Start PowerPoint with a blank

00:05:16.043 --> 00:05:20.563
presentation and pick one theme on the
Design tab, so all three slides match.

00:05:21.643 --> 00:05:23.463
Keep the words few and large.

00:05:25.043 --> 00:05:26.823
Then step back from your screen.

00:05:27.783 --> 00:05:29.191
Can you still read every word?

00:05:30.043 --> 00:05:33.283
If you have to squint,
cut words before you shrink the text.

00:05:35.153 --> 00:05:37.713
Insert the practice
photo on the first slide.

00:05:38.573 --> 00:05:40.697
Cropping trims the edges you do not need,

00:05:40.913 --> 00:05:43.452
so the two people and the
handout fill the frame.

00:05:44.273 --> 00:05:46.708
Resizing changes the size
of the whole picture.

00:05:47.173 --> 00:05:48.581
They solve different problems.

00:05:49.273 --> 00:05:50.071
There is a catch.

00:05:50.973 --> 00:05:52.313
A crop only hides the edges.

00:05:52.973 --> 00:05:57.222
They stay in the file until you compress
the picture and delete the cropped areas.

00:05:58.373 --> 00:05:59.933
Next, add alt text,

00:06:00.873 --> 00:06:04.759
one sentence that says what the photo
shows, for someone who cannot see it.

00:06:05.573 --> 00:06:07.490
PowerPoint may suggest a description.

00:06:07.993 --> 00:06:10.376
Check it the way you
would check an AI answer.

00:06:11.053 --> 00:06:12.333
Then add the credit line.

00:06:13.193 --> 00:06:17.353
Make your three slides, then crop,
describe, and credit the photo.

00:06:18.313 --> 00:06:19.252
Pause the video now.

00:06:33.244 --> 00:06:35.484
Who gets the file decides the format.

00:06:36.344 --> 00:06:40.924
A neighbor who only reads it gets a PDF,
which looks the same on any computer.

00:06:41.744 --> 00:06:44.424
A partner who will edit
it gets the Word document.

00:06:45.604 --> 00:06:48.824
A tool that needs plain
rows of numbers gets a CSV.

00:06:50.164 --> 00:06:53.884
Before you export, choose Review,
then Check Accessibility.

00:06:55.064 --> 00:06:56.519
It points out missing headings,

00:06:56.784 --> 00:06:59.844
missing alt text,
and links that do not say where they go.

00:07:00.544 --> 00:07:04.824
Then choose File, Save As,
and set the type to PDF.

00:07:06.264 --> 00:07:07.884
Saving is not the last step.

00:07:08.744 --> 00:07:11.490
Open the PDF from your folder
and check the headings,

00:07:11.784 --> 00:07:16.204
the numbered steps, and the link, because
that file is what your reader gets.

00:07:17.424 --> 00:07:21.844
Save your handout as a PDF,
open it from the folder, and check it.

00:07:22.724 --> 00:07:23.964
Pause the video now.

00:07:35.139 --> 00:07:37.579
Short how-to videos need editing too.

00:07:38.239 --> 00:07:41.499
Trimming cuts unwanted material
at the start or the end.

00:07:42.319 --> 00:07:44.139
Splitting cuts a clip into pieces,

00:07:44.819 --> 00:07:48.135
so you can remove a section in the middle,
such as a long pause.

00:07:49.639 --> 00:07:51.339
After every cut, play it back.

00:07:52.499 --> 00:07:54.675
Did you clip the first word of a sentence?

00:07:55.139 --> 00:07:57.522
Did you cut the step
that explains the result?

00:07:58.479 --> 00:07:59.759
If the clip has captions,

00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:04.219
check them against the edited sound,
because old captions drift out of time.

00:08:05.559 --> 00:08:08.357
The video editor in Windows
needs a Microsoft account,

00:08:08.899 --> 00:08:11.219
so today you will use a practice page.

00:08:12.079 --> 00:08:14.139
Open the Mission 3D practice page,

00:08:14.879 --> 00:08:18.059
trim the start and end,
then remove the pause in the middle.

00:08:19.299 --> 00:08:20.259
Pause the video now.

00:08:34.368 --> 00:08:37.891
Before you call your pack finished,
use it the way your reader will.

00:08:38.248 --> 00:08:41.808
Open the PDF and find the next
step without hunting for it.

00:08:42.428 --> 00:08:43.178
Click the link.

00:08:43.888 --> 00:08:47.568
In the workbook, change one
cost and watch the total follow.

00:08:48.468 --> 00:08:50.696
Read the second slide
from across the room.

00:08:51.768 --> 00:08:54.208
Each check tests one of today's skills:

00:08:55.028 --> 00:08:56.436
a handout with real structure,

00:08:56.928 --> 00:08:58.008
a formula that updates,

00:08:58.588 --> 00:09:01.848
slides with one message,
and an export you opened yourself.

00:09:03.048 --> 00:09:06.208
Fix the first problem you find,
then check again.

00:09:07.728 --> 00:09:12.528
The same routine works for a club notice,
a church bulletin, or a household budget.

00:09:13.328 --> 00:09:16.388
Make it for a reader, test it,
and keep the original.
